Z||000000||Monkeyman||10-29-2000||07:32 AM||||Anyone know the model # of the all pushbutton stereo?  Can anyone direct me to the thread discussing where to get the keypad parts?  I can't seem to find it anywhere.  A friend of mine just gave me one.  Works about as well as the other one I had.  Powers up fine, but the display only works sometimes and the buttons (volume, etc) don't work.  I wouldn't mind fixing it up if it didn't cost too much.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Seanh||10-29-2000||09:03 AM||||I dont have a club as to the thread location or where you can get the parts. But, when I first got my car, the radio wouldnt work worth a crap, nothing would work, volum, power,seeking. Somtimes it would work find and others nothing. I took it apart, and the problem was a bad on/off switch. Instead of buying a new one and replacing it, since I already was going to put a stereo system in it, I just bypassed the power button. The radio was on when ever the car was on, if you didnt want to hear it, jut turn down the volum. Just a thought, seems to be a common problem with older GM radios.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||10-29-2000||09:56 AM||||MM, is this one you have with or without a cassette? I have an old plain AM/FM factory stereo. If anything will work off this one that might help you out, let me know.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||10-29-2000||12:07 PM||||When my stock radio in my 88 crapped out, I asked Mr. Goodwrench.  They had a repair shop which they sent it to be fixed.  A GM authorized radio repair shop.  Not necessarily cheap, but the radio worked like it should.  Cheaper than an off market replacement am/fm cassette.||||reg||10
Z||000004||Monkeyman||10-29-2000||06:10 PM||||bb--This is the all button cassette (best radio in '85-'87 according to Paul Vargas).  It has (in no order of importance): EQ, seek, scan, music search, auto reverse, AM Stereo switch, CrO2 switch, etc, etc.  Fully loaded for a mid '80s stock unit.<P>If it costs too much to fix, I'll just junk it.  I already have an aftermarket stereo and CD changer for whatever "new" Fiero I get and my sister just gave me the stock cassette radio out of her car (looks like it's the same as the generic AM/FM cassette found in most Fieros--no EQ or music search and 2 knobs instead of buttons).  I can use that until I figure out what I'll be doing with the Grape Ape.  (The idea of putting it under glass in the living room was shot down by my wife. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">)<P>BTW, Doug--It ejects the tape just fine now.  I'll have to hook it up tomorrow to make sure it plays.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||10-30-2000||06:05 PM||||Monkeyman, I have the same radio. I think I might have a manual for it. I've moved twice since the last time I used it, so it might take me a while to dig it out. <BR>You might want to contact  <A HREF="http://www.newellelectronics.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.newellelectronics.com/</A> <BR>They advertise that they do repairs. Might be able to give you a ballpark figure. There's also a place in north Atlanta that works on Delco radios, but they're not cheap. Last I heard, you could still get the pushbutton control panels. but they have black buttons, instead of gray. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>"If I have to fake 'genuine concern' one more time today, I'm gonna scream!" -TCH||||reg||1
